  "title": "CHAPTER 10: TAX STRUCTURE IN INDIA",
  "order": 230,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "u29ty0s",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. MEANING AND OBJECTIVES OF TAXATION"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Adam Smith's four fundamental canons of taxation are Equality, Certainty, Convenience, and Economy.",
        "Direct taxes (like Income Tax and Corporation Tax) cannot be shifted to others, whereas Indirect taxes (like GST) are shiftable to the ultimate consumer.",
        "GST is a destination-based, multi-stage tax levied on value addition at each stage of the supply chain.",
        "In the GST Council, the Central Government holds 1/3rd of the voting power, while all State Governments combined hold 2/3rd of the voting power.",
        "Tax Buoyancy measures the responsiveness of tax revenue growth relative to changes in Gross Domestic Product (GDP)."
      ],
      "id": "4hrncz6"
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
      "stats": [
        {
          "label": "Adam Smith Canons",
          "value": "4 Canons",
          "icon": "book"
        },
        {
          "label": "Primary GST Slabs",
          "value": "5%, 12%, 18%, 28%",
          "icon": "check"
        },
        {
          "label": "GST Implementation",
          "value": "1st July 2017",
          "icon": "calendar"
        },
        {
          "label": "GST Council Decision Majority",
          "value": "3/4th Majority",
          "icon": "award"
        }
      ],
      "id": "bplq45p"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "yqozlf9",
      "content": "Tax is a compulsory contribution imposed by the government on individuals and businesses to raise revenue for public expenditure. It is not a voluntary payment and no direct benefit is guaranteed to the taxpayer."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "wqhedoz",
      "content": "Objectives of Taxation:<br></br>• Raise revenue for government expenditure<br></br>• Reduce income inequality<br></br>• Control inflation<br></br>• Promote economic growth<br></br>• Discourage harmful consumption (sin goods)<br></br>"
    },
    {
      "id": "2yemcfg",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2. CANONS OF TAXATION (ADAM SMITH)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "9bvipi7",
      "content": "• Canon of Equality – Tax should be based on ability to pay.<br></br>• Canon of Certainty – Tax amount and time should be clear.<br></br>• Canon of Convenience – Tax should be easy to pay.<br></br>• Canon of Economy – Cost of collection should be low.<br></br>"
    },
    {
      "id": "ud1s8pt",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "3. CLASSIFICATION OF TAXES"
    },
    {
